Make react-native-change-icon a plain legacy bridge module.

Under RCT_NEW_ARCH_ENABLED the shipped header declares conformance to the
codegen-generated NativeChangeIconSpec, which inherits both RCTBridgeModule and
RCTTurboModule. Advertising RCTTurboModule makes React Native's legacy-module
interop scanner skip the class, but the module is never registered on the
TurboModule side either -- it is absent from the generated RCTModuleProviders.mm.
It therefore falls between the two systems and is never instantiated, so
NativeModules.ChangeIcon is unusable and every call silently does nothing:
the app icon never changes and no error reaches JavaScript.

ChangeIcon.mm compounds the silence. It resolves the promise before calling
setAlternateIconName: and its completion handler discards the NSError, so even a
genuine failure looks like success.

Dropping the #ifdef leaves ChangeIcon a plain RCTBridgeModule, which the interop
layer picks up exactly as it does react-native-device-info and
react-native-zeroconf -- both of which work under the New Architecture precisely
because they declare no codegen spec.

Verified with ModuleSettingsTests.testChangesAppIconToBigOleAndBack, which fails
on the New Architecture without this patch and passes with it, matching the
old-architecture control run.

Upstream is unmaintained for this: 5.0.0 is the latest release and still ships
the broken header.

--- node_modules/react-native-change-icon/ios/ChangeIcon.h
+++ node_modules/react-native-change-icon/ios/ChangeIcon.h
@@ -1,11 +1,6 @@
 #import <UIKit/UIKit.h>

-#ifdef RCT_NEW_ARCH_ENABLED
-#import "RNChangeIconSpec.h"
-@interface ChangeIcon : NSObject <NativeChangeIconSpec, UIApplicationDelegate>
-#else
 #import <React/RCTBridgeModule.h>
 @interface ChangeIcon : NSObject <RCTBridgeModule, UIApplicationDelegate>
-#endif

 @end
